During the COVID-19 pandemic, data-driven analysis played a vital role in pandemic response, often serving multiple purposes simultaneously. As new variants and vaccines emerged, existing models had to adapt to changing circumstances, necessitating adjustments. This research focuses on developing and refining a suite of data-driven analytical tools, leveraging COVID-19 vaccination and case data, to support pandemic response. We have created models for several critical stages: 1) forecasting future vaccine availability and infection rates to determine the optimal time for vaccine booster shots, 2) evaluating the efficacy of vaccine boosters by comparing pre-and post-booster infection rates, 3) determining the maximum safe event attendance by analyzing daily infection and vaccine reports, 4) assessing the impact of the SARS-CoV-2 omicron variant on schools by analyzing infection rates among 5-24 year olds, 5) estimating the long-term effect of vaccination on infection rates, and 6) projecting the long-term effect of vaccination efficacy. Our findings are presented in an easily accessible, pandemic monitoring dashboard, allowing for the quick identification of key insights and trends. This research demonstrates the value of data-driven analysis in supporting pandemic response, and its utility extends beyond the current pandemic, providing a foundation for similar responses to future public health crises. All the code and data used in this research is publicly available at https://github.com/UC-Berkeley-IEOR/COVID-19. The data used in this research is hosted at https://github.com/UC-Berkeley-IEOR/COVID-19/tree/main/data. The results of the research are presented in a video presentation available at https://youtu.be/4kqz2q4j8sA. The authors would like to express their gratitude to the entire UC Berkeley team for their contributions to the project. In particular, the team members who made significant contributions are listed below. For the first part of the research, the 